A recommendation is a category-B-recommendation and is an evidence-type-3-recommendation.
Every nonopioid-therapy shows an at-least-opioid-equal-common-acute-pain-effectiveness.
If a patient has an acute-pain and a nonpharmacologic-therapy is appropriate for the acute-pain and is appropriate for the patient then every clinician should maximize the nonpharmacologic-therapy for the acute-pain.
If a patient has an acute-pain and a nonopioid-pharmacologic-therapy is appropriate for the acute-pain and is appropriate for the patient then every clinician should maximize the nonopioid-pharmacologic-therapy for the acute-pain.
If a patient has an acute-pain and an opioid-benefit does not likely outweigh an opioid-risk for the patient and there is a clinician then it is recommended that the clinician does not consider an opioid-therapy for the acute-pain.
If a patient has an acute-pain and a clinician considers an opioid-prescription for the acute-pain then the clinician should discuss a realistic-benefit with the patient before the opioid-prescription and should discuss a known-risk with the patient before the opioid-prescription.
